## The Core Problem: Unit Mismatch

The JWT `exp` field is in **Unix seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**. This means `Date.now()` is always ~1000x larger than `exp`, so every token appears valid.

```javascript
// BROKEN - Date.now() is milliseconds, exp is seconds
// Date.now() ≈ 1700000000000
// token.exp ≈ 1700000000
// 1700000000000 < 1700000000 is ALWAYS false → never expires

if (Date.now() < decoded.exp) {
    next(); // expired tokens pass through
}
```

## The Fix

```javascript
// CORRECT - convert Date.now() to seconds first
if (Date.now() / 1000 < decoded.exp) {
    next();
}

// OR compare using seconds directly
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) < decoded.exp) {
    next();
}
```

## Complete Robust Middleware

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authenticateToken(req, res, next) {
    const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
    const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1]; // Bearer <token>

    if (!token) {
        return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
    }

    try {
        const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
        // jwt.verify() already checks expiry - but if doing it manually:

        const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

        if (!decoded.exp) {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token has no expiry' });
        }

        if (nowInSeconds >= decoded.exp) {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
        }

        req.user = decoded;
        next();

    } catch (err) {
        // jwt.verify() throws TokenExpiredError automatically
        if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
        }
        if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
            return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
        }
        return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Authentication error' });
    }
}

module.exports = authenticateToken;
```

## Key Recommendations

| Issue | Problem | Solution |
|-------|---------|----------|
| Unit mismatch | `Date.now()` vs `exp` units differ | Divide `Date.now()` by 1000 |
| Manual expiry check | Error-prone, easy to get wrong | Use `jwt.verify()` — it checks expiry automatically |
| No error handling | All JWT errors look the same | Catch `TokenExpiredError` vs `JsonWebTokenError` separately |
| Missing `exp` field | Token never expires | Reject tokens without `exp` |

**The best fix is to let `jwt.verify()` handle expiry automatically** — it's built-in, handles the unit conversion correctly, and throws a specific `TokenExpiredError` you can catch cleanly.
